# DTF tier manifest: backup-exclude-repos  (#2772 / feat/2772-backup-exclude-repos)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The backup archive is written by
# BackupService before any bytes leave the process and is recorded on the
# `backups` row's `storage_path`; the exclude behaviour is backend-agnostic, so
# this runs on the default filesystem overlay and is asserted directly from the
# archive contents (pulled with `docker cp`, extracted with host tar/jq).
#
# The feature (#2772): airgapped / bandwidth-limited deployments want to keep
# specific repositories OUT of full and incremental backups. CreateBackupRequest
# gained an optional `exclude_repositories` (a list of repository ids); when set,
# those repositories' artifacts are omitted from the backup -- both the artifact
# bytes AND their rows in the `database/artifacts.json` table dump inside the
# archive. When omitted (or empty) nothing is excluded, so existing full backups
# are byte-for-byte unchanged. Pre-#2772 the field does not exist, so it is
# ignored: an excluded repository is still fully backed up.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh) is the discriminating gate. Two maven/local repos are
# seeded, "keep" and "drop", each with one artifact:
#   * POST /api/v1/admin/backups {"type":"full","exclude_repositories":[<drop>]}
#     then execute -> the archive's database/artifacts.json contains the "keep"
#     repo's artifact and NOT the "drop" repo's artifact.
#     Pre-#2772: exclude is ignored -> the "drop" artifact is present -> FAIL.
#   * Same assertion for {"type":"incremental",...}.
#   * CONTROL: a backup with NO exclude still contains BOTH artifacts (the
#     feature is additive; default behaviour is unchanged).
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + repo creates,
# artifact uploads, and several admin create/execute-backup calls back to back;
# the base default (ON) could throttle them.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
